@doc """
Configuration for a pool. `create` is the resource factory. The pool
calls it `size` times at startup and once per slot it needs to refill.
The closure carries the concrete resource type, so the pool itself
never needs to know how a resource is built.

`valid` runs on every checked-in resource. When it returns false the
pool drops the resource and refills the slot instead of lending it
again. `None` accepts everything. A database pool passes the driver's
own health predicate here, for example `c -> c.idle?()`.

`destroy` runs on a resource that fails `valid` and on every shelved
resource when the pool stops. A database pool closes the connection
here. `None` drops the value.

When the factory fails, at startup or while refilling a slot, the pool
logs the error and retries on a timer. The first delay is drawn from
`backoff_min_ms` to three times that, each later delay from a range
that grows by up to three times again, and no delay exceeds
`backoff_max_ms`. A success resets the delay. The random spread keeps
pools that start together from retrying together.
"""
struct Config<T>
  backoff_max_ms: Int = 30000
  backoff_min_ms: Int = 1000
  create: fn () -> Result<T, String>
  destroy: Option<fn (T) -> ()> = Option.None
  size: Int = 10
  valid: Option<fn (T) -> Bool> = Option.None
end
